WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:09.199
So far, the workflow has been easy, but sometimes, you need to scan a model from various sides to ensure the best quality.

2
00:00:09.199 --> 00:00:16.359
Failure to do that may give you artifacts in the model and in the texturing, as we saw earlier with the mobile app.

3
00:00:16.359 --> 00:00:25.969
You can scan the model from different sides, based on multiple photo shoots, and then use the software tools to put components together.

4
00:00:25.969 --> 00:00:27.969
Here's how you can do it.

5
00:00:27.969 --> 00:00:45.500
Start a new project the same way as before, by using a set of pictures shot from above the subject.

6
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.500
Align the images together by pressing F6.

7
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:55.590
For the hotkeys to work, ensure the focus is inside the 3D view by clicking or orbiting the 3D window.

8
00:00:55.590 --> 00:01:09.900
Use the top view, 2 on the numerical keypad, and the set ground plane feature to reorient the point cloud to face north.

9
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:18.349
You can also use the reconstruction region to focus on the model.

10
00:01:18.349 --> 00:01:33.060
In this method of work, you need to filter the area of interest by masking out parts that you don't need.

11
00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:42.700
For that, you need a proxy model. It doesn't need to be detailed. Therefore, you can generate a preview mesh, which will be enough.

12
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:59.859
You can further clean it up using the rectangle or lasso tools and the filter selection option, as you have learned to do.

13
00:01:59.859 --> 00:02:07.659
At this stage, you can generate masks that will later help in the process of merging two model parts together.

14
00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:13.360
This is done through the tools menu, using the depth and mask feature.

15
00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:21.060
It is of vital importance that you save those masks in the same folder where the texture maps reside.

16
00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.060
In this case, the lion top folder.

17
00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:33.930
For this process, you don't need camera depth, so you can turn that option off, but ensure the mesh masks option is enabled.

18
00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:44.819
The process is relatively quick, and it creates black and white masks much faster than you would create them in an image processing app like Photoshop.

19
00:02:44.819 --> 00:02:51.219
Next, we do the same with a set of images taken from below the lion's body.

20
00:02:51.219 --> 00:02:57.830
Before you load those pictures, it is important to disable the current cameras in the project.

21
00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:09.340
This can be done by selecting them all using CTRL-A, then disabling them using CTRL-R, and you can deselect them with CTRL-D.

22
00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:12.409
Note that they all turn red in the 3D view.

23
00:03:12.409 --> 00:03:21.340
Next, load the pictures from the lion bottom folder. These represent the photo shoot of the model upside down.

24
00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:46.590
Repeat the process by aligning the images, adjusting the ground plane, and the reconstruction region.

25
00:03:46.590 --> 00:04:11.169
Create a preview model, and clean it up by using the rectangle filter tools, and crop the upside down area, representing the actual top part of the lion model.

26
00:04:11.169 --> 00:04:23.819
Again, you need to create the appropriate mask, this time saving them to the lion bottom folder.

27
00:04:23.819 --> 00:04:36.980
Switch the 2D pane into 1D info pane.

28
00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:47.220
Note that you now have two components, one representing the model seen from above, one from below.

29
00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:54.100
You can even rename these components for more clarity.

30
00:04:54.100 --> 00:05:04.839
Also, regarding the top component, make sure you select all cameras and activate them again.

31
00:05:04.839 --> 00:05:15.129
This is done by using CTRL-A, CTRL-R, and CTRL-D as you did earlier.

32
00:05:15.129 --> 00:05:23.699
To put the two components together, you need to reload the pictures, this time both textures and masks.

33
00:05:23.699 --> 00:05:28.699
Simply drag both top and bottom folders back into RealityCapture.

34
00:05:28.699 --> 00:05:43.180
Once that's done, go ahead and realign the images again.

35
00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:52.180
If all goes well, another single component gets created with a one-point cloud that is the combination of the top and bottom parts.

36
00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:57.399
All cameras are accounted for, from both above and below the model.

37
00:05:57.399 --> 00:06:03.819
The masks ensure a clean result, which you can now generate a normal detailed mesh for,

38
00:06:03.819 --> 00:06:41.029
process it, simplify it, perhaps smooth it a bit, and texture it, ending in a detailed model from very conceivable point of view.

39
00:06:41.029 --> 00:06:49.459
You can finally export it as an FBX or OBJ file to your favorite DCC app or game engine.

40
00:06:49.459 --> 00:06:57.329
Again, this process was fairly easy, but unfortunately it doesn't always work that efficiently.

41
00:06:57.329 --> 00:07:06.329
In the next video, we look at a scenario where this process fails and how you can overcome the problem when it happens.
